
## How pasted images are named and typed

A pasted image has no file name, so the SDK names it `image-<attachmentId>`. Its listed metadata comes from a format hint Teams sends with the message:

- `Name` gets an extension only for PNG, JPEG, GIF, or WebP, and `Extension` is taken from it, so it is absent when the name has none.
- `ContentType` is the matching MIME type for those four formats, otherwise `application/octet-stream`.
- `UniqueId` is always unset, because a pasted image is not stored in OneDrive or SharePoint.
- `ContentUrl` is the image's Bot API location.

On the downloaded file, `ContentType` is detected from the bytes, and the extension of `Filename` follows it, so the downloaded file name can differ from the listed name. If the bytes are not PNG, JPEG, GIF, or WebP, the listed type is used.

:::note
Teams saves a pasted GIF as a static PNG. It is listed as `.gif` and `image/gif` but downloads as a PNG.
:::

## Formats that cannot be downloaded

Some image formats, such as BMP and AVIF, cannot be converted by the Teams service, so Teams shows a placeholder, and they cannot be downloaded. Downloading one of these fails with `InlineImageTransformationException`. The same image sent as a file attachment downloads normally, so asking the user to resend it that way is the recovery:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Teams.Apps.Files;

try
{
    DownloadedFile downloaded = await image.DownloadAsync(cancellationToken);
    // ...
}
catch (InlineImageTransformationException)
{
    await context.ReplyAsync("That pasted image cannot be downloaded. Try sending it as a file attachment instead.", cancellationToken);
}
```

If the Bot API refuses the token presented for the image, the download fails with `InlineImageAccessException` with a `401` or `403` status. Both errors share the common base error described in [Handle errors](#receiving-files).

## How the image is read

A pasted image is read from the Bot API with the same token the app uses to reply in that conversation, not through Microsoft Graph. This works for traditional bots and for agentic users, which read as their own identity. Inline images list and download in personal chats, group chats, and channels. Outside personal chats, only messages that @mention the agent reach it.

The token is sent only to a URL under the activity's `serviceUrl`. An inline image hosted anywhere else is skipped by `ListAsync()` with a warning in the log.

## Access the raw attachments

An inbound message with a pasted image typically contains two related attachments:

```json
{
  "attachments": [
    {
      "contentType": "image/png",
      "contentUrl": "https://.../v3/attachments/{id}/views/original"
    },
    {
      "contentType": "text/html",
      "content": "<p><img src=\"https://.../objects/{id}/views/imgo\" ...></p>"
    }
  ]
}
```

The listed file's `Raw` holds only the `image/*` attachment. The `text/html` attachment, which preserves where the image appeared among the message's text and other content, stays in `context.Activity.Attachments`.

---

### PAGE: Receiving and sending messages

# Receiving and sending messages

Configure the blueprint client ID, credential, and tenant ID in your app's SDK credentials. Teams SDK uses them to acquire tokens for the Agentic User.

## Reactive messaging

An inbound Agent 365 activity includes the Agentic User in `activity.recipient`. The handler context automatically uses that identity for replies, sends, and API calls.

```csharp
teamsApp.OnMessage(async (context, cancellationToken) =>
{
    AgenticIdentity? agenticIdentity =
        context.Activity.Recipient?.GetAgenticIdentity();

    if (agenticIdentity?.AgenticUserId is not string agenticUserId)
    {
        throw new InvalidOperationException(
            "The activity is not addressed to an Agentic User.");
    }

    await context.ReplyAsync(
        $"Hi! I'm an Agentic User, and my user ID is {agenticUserId}. Nice to meet you!",
        cancellationToken);
});
```

:::note Who sends the reply?
The example sends the reply as the Agentic User from the inbound activity, not as the blueprint. The blueprint authenticates the reusable runtime, but it isn't the conversational sender.
:::

## Bot capabilities

Most Teams capabilities available to standard bots are also available to Agentic Users, including cards, reactions, and API calls. For inbound activities, the handler's API client uses the Agentic User identity automatically.

This example adds a reaction to an incoming message as the Agentic User:

```csharp
teamsApp.OnMessage(async (context, cancellationToken) =>
{
    await context.Api.Conversations.AddReactionAsync(
        context.Activity.Conversation.Id,
        context.Activity.Id,
        ReactionType.Like,
        cancellationToken: cancellationToken
    );
});
```

For more reaction options, see [Message Reactions](#message-reactions).

## Proactive messaging

A proactive operation has no inbound activity that identifies the sender. Create an `AgenticIdentity` associated with the Agentic User, then pass it as the operation scope. The app helper supplies the required blueprint ID from app configuration; the Agentic App Instance and Agentic User IDs select the user-backed scope used by this example.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  R[Reactive activity] --> RI[SDK reads Agentic User<br/>from recipient]
  RI --> RS[reply / send / API call]
  P[Proactive job] --> PI[App supplies Agentic User]
  PI --> PS[AgenticIdentity-scoped<br/>send / API call]
```

If your app will send proactive messages later, store the conversation ID, service URL, Agentic App Instance ID, Agentic User ID, and tenant ID in persistent storage when it receives an activity.

```csharp
var agenticIdentity = new AgenticIdentity
{
    AgenticAppId = agenticAppId,
    AgenticUserId = agenticUserId,
    AgenticAppBlueprintId = blueprintId,
    TenantId = tenantId
};

await teamsApp.SendAsync(
    conversationId,
    "Your scheduled update is ready.",
    serviceUrl,
    agenticIdentity,
    cancellationToken);

ApiClient api = teamsApp.Api
    .ForServiceUrl(serviceUrl)
    .ForAgenticIdentity(agenticIdentity);

await api.Conversations.AddReactionAsync(
    conversationId,
    activityId,
    ReactionType.Like,
    cancellationToken: cancellationToken);
```

